page_context[1]:
  - title: "Delete your account"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/account-deletion/"
    date: "0001-01-01T00:00:00Z"
    category: "page"
    description: "How to ask Runink to delete your account and the data tied to it, for each Runink app."
    content: "This page explains how to ask for your account to be deleted in each app Runink publishes. Where an app cannot delete an account by itself, deletion is done on request.\nFACE (org.runink.face) FACE is a business console. Your organisation\u0026rsquo;s administrator adds you, and can remove you.\nIn the app: Settings → Account → Your data → Delete my account. Type your own email address to confirm.\nDeleted:\nyour sign-in account: email address, password hash, two-factor secret and role; every session of yours, on every device, which end at once; the follow-up flows you own, with their runs, their chat and the proposals made for you; the scenarios, items and terms you typed into the scenario builder; the mailbox connections you started and did not finish; the brief suggestions you dismissed; on your device: the cached data and your saved role. You are signed out on every device first, then your data is deleted, then the account record. Earlier encrypted versions of these records stay in FACE\u0026rsquo;s storage history until it is compacted and cleaned; nobody can read them without FACE\u0026rsquo;s key. A personal brief you saved keeps a code derived from your account and is not deleted yet.\nWhat your organisation keeps: what belongs to your organisation is not yours personally and stays: briefs, personas, connections, schedules, the run history, the mailbox your organisation connected, the follow-up tasks flows created, the audit log and the decision records. You are not named in them afterwards: where a retained record names you, it shows a fixed code, \u0026ldquo;former-member-\u0026rdquo; and ten characters, instead (the same code for the same person every time). The decision log is a hash chain and is not rewritten: its stored entries keep an anonymous account number that no longer links to any account or email address. One audit entry records that the deletion happened (when, which classes of data, a hash standing for the account), with no content and no address.\nNot reached by the in-app deletion: an older copy of FACE\u0026rsquo;s sign-in table, from before FACE moved its storage, is keyed by your email address and is not reached by the in-app deletion. An operator removes it on request: write to privacy@runink.org from your sign-in address. Also not rewritten: fetch run history, archive marks, trend-topic exclusions and uploaded-document manifests (they keep the opaque account number, not an email).\nAfter the deletion: FACE\u0026rsquo;s access list is built from add-users requests that FACE itself cannot change, so deleting your account does not remove your address from it. If it is still listed, signing in with Google again creates a new, empty account; ask your organisation\u0026rsquo;s administrator to remove you.\nWithout the app: email privacy@runink.org from your sign-in address with the subject \u0026ldquo;Delete my FACE account\u0026rdquo;. For a copy of your data first, use the subject \u0026ldquo;Export my FACE data\u0026rdquo; (there is no in-app export).\nThe FACE privacy policy describes everything FACE stores.\nPULSE (org.runink.pulse) PULSE is a marketing workspace your organisation buys per seat. You sign in with Google.\nIn the app: Settings › Delete my account. Type your account\u0026rsquo;s email to confirm. PULSE signs you out everywhere, then deletes your account and your data in PULSE, including the files you own: uploaded logos and brand assets, avatar images and voice samples, generated creatives, podcasts, audiobooks and videos, and files attached to your audits. Files made for an agency client belong to that client and are deleted when the client\u0026rsquo;s account is deleted. A deployment\u0026rsquo;s only admin must make another member an admin first.\nSome copies stay for now: earlier versions in storage history, and entries in append-only logs. To have those erased, email privacy@runink.org from the address you sign in with, with the subject \u0026ldquo;Delete my PULSE account\u0026rdquo;. If you cannot sign in, use the same address.\nKept: posts and messages already published stay on the networks they were sent to (LinkedIn, Instagram, YouTube, email, WhatsApp, SMS); Runink cannot remove them there.\nDeleting your account does not remove your access: signing in with Google again creates a new, empty account. The PULSE privacy policy describes everything PULSE stores.\nLuna (org.runink.luna) Luna has no delete button in the app today. Deletion is done by an operator on request.\nTo ask for deletion: email privacy@runink.org from the Google account you sign in to Luna with, with the subject \u0026ldquo;Delete my Luna account\u0026rdquo;. We answer within 30 days. Your access to Luna (the allowlist) and your data are separate: you can ask for either or both.\nWhat Luna stores is described in the Luna privacy policy.\nTIDE (org.runink.tide) In the app: Settings › User › Delete my account: type your e-mail to confirm. All your sessions end first, then TIDE deletes your personas, your briefs (including briefs sent to you), your seat, your admin role, and your project chats and Ask TIDE history.\nSome records are kept: the audit log, the records of a subscription you bought, and organisation records that name you as the person who acted. These are encrypted at rest. If you bought a subscription that is still active, end it in Manage billing first.\nYour organisation\u0026rsquo;s access grant stays until your administrator removes it. Earlier versions in storage history, and entries in append-only logs, stay until they are erased; to have them erased, email privacy@runink.org from the address you sign in with.\nThe TIDE privacy policy describes what TIDE stores.\nQuestions about any of this: privacy@runink.org.\n"
